// LONGEST run fastest; short waves are carried by surface tension and the SHORTEST run
// fastest. Between the two the group velocity passes through a minimum, and that is why a
// raindrop ring looks the way it does: hair-fine capillary ripples race out AHEAD of the
// swell they were born with, while the long gravity wake trails behind.
//
// Rendered by stationary phase rather than by stepping a wave equation. At radius r and age
// tau the only wavenumbers that have arrived are those whose GROUP velocity equals r/tau —
// and because c_g has a minimum, there are exactly TWO of them at every point (one gravity,
// one capillary) or, inside the minimum-group-velocity radius, none at all, which is why the
// water behind the ring goes quiet again. A 256-entry table inverts c_g(k) = v on each
// branch once per parameter change, so each pixel costs two cosines per drop, not a spectrum.
// A wave equation on a grid cannot do this at all: it is non-dispersive, every wavelength
// travels at one speed, and the ripples could never outrun the swell.
